<subsection xml:id="subsec-double-integrals">
  <title>Double Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The definite integral of a single-variable function measures the signed area
      enclosed between the graph and the horizontal axis.
      We now generalise this idea to functions of two variables: instead of an area,
      the integral will measure a <em>volume</em>, and instead of an interval, the
      domain of integration is a planar region.
      The construction follows the same Riemann-sum logic — approximate by simple
      pieces, then pass to the limit.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Univariate Riemann sum review ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-riemann-sum-univariate">
    <title>Review: Univariate Integrals as Riemann Sums</title>
    <p>
      For a continuous function <m>f\colon [a,b] \to \R</m>, the definite integral
      <me>
        \int_a^b f(x) \, dx
      </me>
      measures the signed area enclosed between the graph of <m>f</m> and the
      <m>x</m>-axis.
      It is defined as the limit of <em>Riemann sums</em>: subdivide <m>[a,b]</m>
      into <m>n</m> sub-intervals of equal length <m>\Delta x_i = \tfrac{b-a}{n}</m>,
      choose a representative point <m>x_i</m> in each sub-interval, and sum the
      rectangle areas:
      <me>
        \sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\,\Delta x_i
        \;\xrightarrow{\;n \to \infty\;}\;
        \int_a^b f(x) \, dx.
      </me>
      Each term <m>f(x_i)\,\Delta x_i</m> is the area of a thin rectangle of
      height <m>f(x_i)</m> and width <m>\Delta x_i</m>.
      As the subdivision is refined, the approximation converges to the exact integral.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-riemann-sum-1d">
      <caption>
        Approximating <m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m> by Riemann sum rectangles (shown in
        blue). As the number of rectangles increases the total rectangle area
        converges to the exact integral.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-riemann-sum-1d" width="60%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
            \fill[blue!20] (0.5,0) rectangle (1.0,1.20);
            \fill[blue!20] (1.0,0) rectangle (1.5,2.08);
            \fill[blue!20] (1.5,0) rectangle (2.0,2.81);
            \fill[blue!20] (2.0,0) rectangle (2.5,2.71);
            \fill[blue!20] (2.5,0) rectangle (3.0,1.90);
            \fill[blue!20] (3.0,0) rectangle (3.5,1.06);
            \draw[blue!60] (0.5,0) rectangle (1.0,1.20);
            \draw[blue!60] (1.0,0) rectangle (1.5,2.08);
            \draw[blue!60] (1.5,0) rectangle (2.0,2.81);
            \draw[blue!60] (2.0,0) rectangle (2.5,2.71);
            \draw[blue!60] (2.5,0) rectangle (3.0,1.90);
            \draw[blue!60] (3.0,0) rectangle (3.5,1.06);
            \draw[thick, blue!80!black]
              plot[smooth, domain=0.5:3.5, samples=60]
              (\x, {2.5*exp(-(\x-2.2)*(\x-2.2)/1.3)+0.38})
              node[right] {$f(x)$};
            \fill[blue!80!black] (1.0,1.20) circle (1.2pt);
            \fill[blue!80!black] (1.5,2.08) circle (1.2pt);
            \fill[blue!80!black] (2.0,2.81) circle (1.2pt);
            \fill[blue!80!black] (2.5,2.71) circle (1.2pt);
            \fill[blue!80!black] (3.0,1.90) circle (1.2pt);
            \fill[blue!80!black] (3.5,1.06) circle (1.2pt);
            \draw[->,thick] (0.2,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0.5,-0.15) -- (0.5,3.25) node[above] {$y$};
            \node[below] at (0.5,-0.02) {$a$};
            \node[below] at (3.5,-0.02) {$b$};
            \draw[latex-latex] (0.5,-0.52) -- (1.0,-0.52)
              node[midway, below, font=\small] {$\Delta x_i$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Volume motivation ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-double-integral-volume-motivation">
    <title>Volume Between a Bivariate Function and the <m>xy</m>-Plane</title>
    <p>
      Now let <m>f\colon D_f \to \R</m> be a continuous function of two variables,
      and let <m>A \subset D_f</m> be a bounded planar domain.
      We want to compute the <em>volume</em> of the solid region lying between
      the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> and the <m>xy</m>-plane above <m>A</m>.
      This volume is called the <em>double integral</em>:
      <me>
        \iint\limits_A f(x,y) \, dA,
      </me>
      where <m>A</m> is the domain of integration and <m>f(x,y)</m> is the integrand.
    </p>
    <p>
      The construction mirrors the univariate case.
      Subdivide <m>A</m> into <m>n</m> small sub-domains with areas
      <m>\Delta A_1, \ldots, \Delta A_n</m>.
      Over each sub-domain <m>A_k</m>, approximate the solid by a
      <em>vertical cylinder with a flat top</em>: choose any point
      <m>(x_k, y_k) \in A_k</m> and form the approximate volume
      <me>
        \Delta V_k = f(x_k, y_k)\cdot\Delta A_k.
      </me>
      Summing over all sub-domains gives the Riemann sum approximation:
      <me>
        \iint\limits_A f(x,y)\,dA
        \;\approx\;
        \sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k,y_k)\cdot\Delta A_k.
      </me>
      Refining the subdivision indefinitely (so that each <m>\Delta A_k \to 0</m>)
      makes this sum converge to the exact double integral.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Definition ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-double-integral">
    <title>Double Integral in <m>\R^2</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A \subset \R^2</m> be a bounded domain and <m>f\colon A \to \R</m>
        a continuous function.
        For sub-domain areas <m>\Delta A_k</m> and representative points
        <m>(x_k, y_k) \in A_k</m> as described above, the
        <term>double integral</term> of <m>f</m> over <m>A</m> is defined as:
        <me>
          \iint\limits_A f(x,y)\,dA
          \;=\;
          \lim_{\substack{n \to \infty \\ \Delta A_k \to 0}}
          \sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k,y_k)\,\Delta A_k.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-double-integral-existence">
    <p>
      This limit does not exist for all functions and all domains.
      In this course we work exclusively with continuous functions on
      bounded domains for which the double integral is well-defined.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Applications ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-double-integral-applications-transition">
    <title>Three Basic Applications of Double Integrals</title>
    <p>
      We now start a new line of thought. After defining the double integral as
      a Riemann-sum limit, we use the same idea in three concrete applications:
      computing the area of a planar domain, locating its geometric center, and
      accumulating a variable surface density such as electric charge. In each
      case, the double integral adds up a local contribution over all small area
      elements of the domain.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-area-double-integral">
    <title>Area of a Planar Domain</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>area</term> <m>F(A)</m> of a planar domain <m>A \subseteq \R^2</m>
        is obtained by integrating the constant function <m>1</m>:
        <me>
          F(A) = \iint\limits_A 1\,dA.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-area-double-integral">
    <caption>
      The formula <m>F(A)=\iint_A 1\,dA</m> adds one unit of area density over
      every infinitesimal area element <m>dA</m> in the planar domain <m>A</m>.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-area-double-integral" width="55%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, font=\small]
          \fill[green!18]
            (0.4,0.5) .. controls (0.7,1.8) and (1.5,2.3) .. (2.8,2.0)
            .. controls (3.5,1.8) and (3.8,0.8) .. (3.0,0.35)
            .. controls (2.1,-0.1) and (0.9,0.0) .. (0.4,0.5);
          \draw[thick, green!45!black]
            (0.4,0.5) .. controls (0.7,1.8) and (1.5,2.3) .. (2.8,2.0)
            .. controls (3.5,1.8) and (3.8,0.8) .. (3.0,0.35)
            .. controls (2.1,-0.1) and (0.9,0.0) .. (0.4,0.5);
          \foreach \x in {0.9,1.4,1.9,2.4,2.9}
            \draw[green!55!black, opacity=0.45] (\x,0.35) -- (\x,2.0);
          \foreach \y in {0.6,1.0,1.4,1.8}
            \draw[green!55!black, opacity=0.45] (0.6,\y) -- (3.25,\y);
          \fill[green!45!black] (1.85,1.2) circle (1pt);
          \node at (2.0,1.15) {$dA$};
          \node at (2.2,2.45) {$A$};
          \node[align=center] at (2.0,-0.55)
            {$\displaystyle F(A)=\iint_A 1\,dA$};
          \draw[->,thick] (0.0,0) -- (4.1,0) node[right] {$x$};
          \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,2.6) node[above] {$y$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <definition xml:id="def-geometric-center-2d">
    <title>Geometric Center of a Planar Domain</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Integrating the coordinate functions <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> over <m>A</m>
        gives the total coordinate moments of the domain. Dividing these moments
        by the total area <m>F(A)</m> yields the average coordinates of all area
        elements, that is, the coordinates of the <term>geometric center</term>
        (centroid) of <m>A</m>:
        <me>
          x_C = \frac{1}{F(A)}\iint\limits_A x\,dA,
          \qquad
          y_C = \frac{1}{F(A)}\iint\limits_A y\,dA.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-geometric-center-2d">
    <caption>
      The geometric center <m>C=(x_C,y_C)</m> is the balance point of a planar
      domain with uniform density. The integrals of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> compute
      coordinate moments; dividing by the total area <m>F(A)</m> turns these
      moments into average coordinates.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-geometric-center-2d" width="55%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, font=\small]
          \fill[orange!18]
            (0.45,0.45) .. controls (0.8,1.6) and (1.7,2.25) .. (2.75,1.9)
            .. controls (3.45,1.65) and (3.55,0.65) .. (2.85,0.25)
            .. controls (1.95,-0.25) and (0.8,-0.05) .. (0.45,0.45);
          \draw[thick, orange!65!black]
            (0.45,0.45) .. controls (0.8,1.6) and (1.7,2.25) .. (2.75,1.9)
            .. controls (3.45,1.65) and (3.55,0.65) .. (2.85,0.25)
            .. controls (1.95,-0.25) and (0.8,-0.05) .. (0.45,0.45);
          \coordinate (C) at (1.95,1.0);
          \draw[dashed, orange!70!black] (C) -- (1.95,0);
          \draw[dashed, orange!70!black] (C) -- (0,1.0);
          \draw[thick, orange!80!black] (1.83,1.0) -- (2.07,1.0);
          \draw[thick, orange!80!black] (1.95,0.88) -- (1.95,1.12);
          \fill[orange!90!black] (C) circle (1.5pt);
          \node[above left] at (C) {$C=(x_C,y_C)$};
          \node[below] at (1.95,0) {$x_C$};
          \node[left] at (0,1.0) {$y_C$};
          \node at (3.0,1.45) {$A$};
          \draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (4.0,0) node[right] {$x$};
          \draw[->,thick] (0,-0.15) -- (0,2.45) node[above] {$y$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <!-- ─── Physics motivation: surface charge ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-surface-charge-setup">
    <title>Surface Charge Density</title>
    <p>
      In physics, many quantities are specified as <em>local densities</em> that
      only yield a global value after integration.
      For a charged conductive plane surface <m>A</m>, the distribution of electric
      charge is described by the <em>charge density</em> <m>\sigma(x,y)</m>,
      where <m>\sigma(x,y)</m> is the charge per unit area at the point
      <m>(x,y) \in A</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To find the total charge <m>q</m> on the entire surface, one integrates
      <m>\sigma</m> over <m>A</m>:
      <me>
        q = \iint\limits_A \sigma(x,y)\,dA.
      </me>
      We will return to this example in <xref ref="ex-surface-charge-polar"/>
      once we have the tools to evaluate the integral explicitly.
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-double-integrals-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-double-integral-area-meaning">
      <title>Meaning of <m>\iint_D 1\,dA</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does the double integral
          <me>
            \iint_D 1\,dA
          </me>
          represent?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>The area of the domain <m>D</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. Integrating the constant density <m>1</m> over <m>D</m> gives its area.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The perimeter of <m>D</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Perimeters are line-integral quantities, not double integrals.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The geometric center of <m>D</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. A centroid needs moment integrals as well.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The slope of the boundary of <m>D</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. A double integral does not describe boundary slopes.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-double-integral-rectangle-computation">
      <title>Compute a Simple Double Integral</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute
          <me>
            \int_0^1\int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy\,dx.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First integrate with respect to <m>y</m>:
          <me>
            \int_0^2 (x+y)\,dy
            =
            \left[xy+\frac{y^2}{2}\right]_0^2
            =
            2x+2.
          </me>
          Then integrate with respect to <m>x</m>:
          <me>
            \int_0^1 (2x+2)\,dx
            =
            \left[x^2+2x\right]_0^1
            = 3.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
